        "eyebrow": "Virtual staging near you",
        "heading": "Virtual Staging Seattle, WA",
        "lede": "Virtual staging is the process of furnishing a listing's photos instead of the property: an interior designer adds realistic 3D furniture and decor to images of empty or cleared rooms, so buyers see how the home lives without physical staging. Grey skies and empty rooms are a hard combination to sell, and Bella's designers stage Seattle and Eastside listings in 24 to 48 hours, from {price:virtual-staging} a photo.",
        "cta_label": "Stage my listing — from {price:virtual-staging}",
        "cta_link": "/products/virtual-staging",
        "alt_label": "Browse the lookbook",
        "alt_link": "/pages/lookbook",
        "city": "Seattle",
        "market_heading": "Why virtual staging matters in the Seattle market",
        "market_text": "<p>Seattle and the Eastside run on tech relocation. Buyers arriving for roles in Bellevue, Redmond and South Lake Union are often house-hunting from another city, sometimes another country, on a start-date deadline. The listing photos are the entire tour until the week they land.</p><p>The light is a genuine technical problem here for much of the year. An empty white-walled condo photographed under overcast Puget Sound skies has almost no warmth in it, and no amount of exposure correction adds the furniture, texture and colour that make a room feel lived in. Home staging is what puts that back.</p><p>Bellevue and downtown Seattle also skew heavily to towers, where a one-bedroom unit photographs like every other one-bedroom in the building. When the floor plan is shared by forty other listings, the staging is the only thing distinguishing yours in the scroll.</p><p>Buyers who can't tour in person lean on the listing, and most Seattle buyers start their search online: 97% of buyers use the internet in their home search, according to the National Association of Realtors. For relocating tech buyers especially, Seattle virtual staging gives them warm, furnished listing photos matched to the expectations of each neighborhood, without thousands a month in furniture rental.</p><p>Empty homes look smaller and less inviting than they are, and in a rainy city, buyers tend to vet listings online before committing to a wet Saturday of showings. Staging helps them visualize the property's size and potential, shows a room's dimensions and layout at a glance, and it can illustrate a potential use — a spare bedroom shown as a home office or a guest room, a reading nook on a landing — with furniture styles that match local taste, from Scandinavian minimalism to warm Pacific Northwest modern.</p>",
        "local_heading": "Seattle virtual staging for every neighborhood",
        "local_text": "<p>Designers stage clean contemporary for Bellevue, downtown and South Lake Union towers, Pacific Northwest warmth for Ballard, Fremont and Capitol Hill character homes, and family looks for Kirkland, Redmond, Sammamish and Mercer Island.</p><p>Compact tower floor plans get furnished to scale rather than flattered. At Eastside price per square foot, buyers measure carefully, and staging that oversells a room loses the trust it was meant to build. Files come back NWMLS-ready in 24 to 48 hours.</p><p>Every image is clearly identified as virtually staged, with disclosure guidance your broker can review and full usage rights for the MLS, your website and social. Volume discounts apply automatically — 5% at 3+ photos, 10% at 6+, 15% at 11+ and 20% at 21+.</p>",
        "hoods_label": "Where we stage most",
        "hoods": "Bellevue, Kirkland, Redmond, Capitol Hill, Ballard, Fremont, Queen Anne, South Lake Union, Mercer Island, Sammamish"
      }
    },
