    "faq": {
      "type": "bella-faq",
      "blocks": {
        "q1": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "My photos were shot on a grey day and everything looks cold. Can staging fix that?",
            "answer": "That's a large part of what it does here. Furniture, texture and colour put warmth into a room that overcast Puget Sound light strips out — and where the exterior needs help, day-to-dusk editing is available too."
          }
        },
        "q2": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Will these upload to NWMLS?",
            "answer": "Yes. Files come back at standard NWMLS dimensions and specs. Disclosure wording is included and we recommend noting virtual staging in the listing remarks."
          }
        },
        "q3": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "My buyer is relocating for a tech role and hasn't visited. Does that change anything?",
            "answer": "It raises the stakes on photos and on turnaround. A buyer moving for a start date house-hunts inside a narrow window from another city, so the listing needs to be photograph-ready the day it goes live."
          }
        },
        "q4": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "It's a 550 sq ft Bellevue one-bedroom. Is staging going to look ridiculous?",
            "answer": "Not if it's done to scale, which is the point. The buyer wants to know whether a real bed and a real sofa fit — furniture that oversizes the room fails that test at the showing, so we furnish honestly."
          }
        },
        "q5": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How much does it cost to stage a home in Seattle?",
            "answer": "Physical staging in Seattle is quoted per home — size, number of rooms and rental length all move the price. As a general benchmark, conventional staging runs about $2,000–5,000 a month until the home sells. Virtual staging from Bella is {price:virtual-staging} a photo, paid once, with no rental to extend if the listing sits."
          }
        },
        "q6": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How much do people charge for virtual staging?",
            "answer": "Prices vary by provider and by whether a person or an AI app does the work. Bella's designer-made virtual staging is {price:virtual-staging} per photo, with volume discounts of 5% at 3+ photos, 10% at 6+, 15% at 11+ and 20% at 21+. Furniture removal for occupied rooms, unlimited revisions for two weeks and full usage rights are included — no subscription, no minimum."
          }
        },
        "q7": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Is virtual staging worth it? Will it sell my home faster?",
            "answer": "For most vacant or dated listings, yes. Staging can't guarantee a sale, but 97% of buyers use the internet in their home search, according to the National Association of Realtors, so the photos decide which homes get a showing. NAR's research on home staging also finds that staging makes it easier for buyers to visualize a property as their future home. In Seattle, staged photos give an empty or grey-day listing the warmth buyers expect, for a one-time cost per photo instead of monthly furniture rental."
          }
        },
        "q8": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Is virtual staging legal in Washington? Do I have to disclose it?",
            "answer": "Yes, it's legal, and yes, disclose it. MLS rules generally prohibit misleading listing photos and the NAR Code of Ethics requires a true picture in advertising, so label each virtually staged image and note virtual staging in the listing remarks. Add furniture and decor only — never alter structural elements or hide defects — and it's good practice to include an unstaged photo of major rooms. Check NWMLS rules for exact labeling; every Bella order includes disclosure guidance."
          }
        },
        "q9": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Can you show a room in more than one style, or a home that isn't built yet?",
            "answer": "Yes. Because the furniture is digital, a room can be staged in more than one Look Book style, which makes it easy to test which look suits the buyer. For dated rooms, virtual renovation (from {price:virtual-renovation-service}) shows new floors, walls and finishes; for homes that aren't built yet, 3D rendering (from {price:3d-rendering}) creates photorealistic images from plans."
          }
        },
        "q10": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How can I virtually stage my home for free?",
            "answer": "Free AI apps and DIY software can drop furniture into a photo, but the results often distort scale and finishes, and a misleading image creates disclosure problems. New clients can claim a $40 voucher on Bella's sign-up page to try designer-made staging, subject to Bella's approval."
          }
        }
      },
